'''vooga-hole''' (vö′ga-hōl), ''n.'' Same as ''vug.''

'''voracious''' (vọ̄-rā′shus), ''a.'' [= F. ''vorace'' = Sp. Pg. ''voraz'' = It. ''vorace'', < L. ''vorax'' (''vorac-''), swallowing greedily, ravenous, < ''vorare'', swallow, devour; cf. Gr. √ βορ in βορά, food, βρῶμα, food (see ''broma''), βιβρώσκειν, eat, Skt. √ ''gar'', swallow. Cf. ''vorant'', ''devour''.] '''1.''' Greedy in eating; eating food in large quantities; marked by voracity; ravenous: as, a ''voracious'' man.
{{smaller block|I have seen of the king carrion crows. . . . They are very ''voracious'', and will despatch a carcass in a trice.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, an. 1676.}}
They are men of a ''voracious'' appetite, but no taste.
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 452.}}}}
'''2.''' Rapacious.
{{smaller block|I would have removed this defect, and formed no ''voracious'' or destructive animals, which only prey on the other parts of the creation.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Asem.}}
Confess to me, as the first proof of it [confidence], didst thou never shrink back from so ''voracious'' and intractable a monster as that accursed snake?
{{right|''Landor'', Imag. Conv., Alexander and the Priest of [Hammon.}}}}
'''3.''' Ready to swallow up: as, a ''voracious'' gulf or whirlpool. ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Ravenous'', etc. See ''rapacious''.}}

'''voraciously''' (vọ̄-rā′shus-li), ''adv.'' In a voracious manner; with greedy appetite; ravenously; rapaciously.

'''voraciousness''' (vọ̄-rā′shus-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being voracious; greediness of appetite; ravenousness; voracity.

